✅ 15. Interrupt a Sleeping Thread

15.1 Definition

🔴 interrupt() is a method of the Thread class used to request that a thread stop what it is doing.
🔴 Important: interrupt() does not forcibly stop a thread.
🔴 Instead, it signals the thread by setting its interrupt status (interrupt flag).
🔴 If the thread is blocked in an interruptible method like sleep(), wait(), or join(), that method throws InterruptedException.

15.2 Method Signature
public void interrupt()

15.3 Why Do We Need interrupt()?
Imagine a worker thread:

Download File
↓
Sleep 10 Seconds
↓
Continue

Now suppose the user clicks Cancel Download.

Should we wait 10 seconds?
❌ No.

Instead
workerThread.interrupt();
The sleeping thread wakes up immediately.

15.4 How interrupt() Works

Suppose
Thread.sleep(10000);

Current State
TIMED_WAITING

Another thread executes
thread.interrupt();

Flow

TIMED_WAITING
↓
interrupt()
↓
InterruptedException
↓
Catch Block
↓
Continue / Exit

15.5 Output
Thread Sleeping...

Thread Interrupted!

Notice
We never waited

10 seconds
The thread woke immediately because it was interrupted.

15.6 Step-by-Step Explanation

Step 1
Main creates thread.
thread.start();

State
RUNNABLE

Step 2

Child executes
Thread.sleep(10000);

State
TIMED_WAITING

Step 3

Main waits
Thread.sleep(2000);

to ensure child is sleeping.

Step 4

Main executes
thread.interrupt();
This interrupts the sleeping thread.

Step 5

JVM notices

Sleeping Thread
+
Interrupt Signal

Immediately throws
InterruptedException
inside the child thread.

Step 6

Execution jumps directly to
catch (InterruptedException e)

Output

Thread Interrupted!
The remaining code after sleep() is skipped.

