15.8 What is the Interrupt Flag?

Every thread internally has an interrupt status flag.

Initially
false

When another thread executes
thread.interrupt();

the flag becomes
true

However, if the thread is blocked in sleep(), wait(), or join(), the JVM:

Throws InterruptedException
Clears the interrupt flag (sets it back to false)

This is an important interview point.

15.9. interrupt() vs stop()

| Feature                 | `interrupt()` | `stop()`          |
| ----------------------- | ------------- | ----------------- |
| Safe                    | ✅ Yes         | ❌ No (deprecated) |
| Graceful                | ✅ Yes         | ❌ No              |
| Cooperative             | ✅ Yes         | ❌ No              |
| Releases Locks Abruptly | ❌ No          | ✅ Yes (dangerous) |

15.10 interrupt() vs sleep()
| Feature                       | `interrupt()`        | `sleep()`                             |
| ----------------------------- | -------------------- | ------------------------------------- |
| Purpose                       | Request interruption | Pause current thread                  |
| Static                        | ❌ No                 | ✅ Yes                                 |
| Throws `InterruptedException` | No                   | Yes (when interrupted while sleeping) |
| Changes State                 | Signals thread       | Causes `TIMED_WAITING`                |

